# Erdos Problem #99 - phase-star rhombic train gap 0.0018

**Date:** 2026-09-14
**Repository base:** active `research/c2-closure-sol-20260914-r5` branch
**State version:** `2026-09-14.audit.51`
**Run type:** quantitative closing-proof reduction / dominant-orbit phase-star stability
**Target:** Target C-II, canonical full-core Type-II branch
**Claim class:** proved candidate awaiting independent audit
**External literature:** no new import
**Computation:** dependency-free outward constant regression only

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

The preceding sharpened rhombic-train note improved the explicit full-core floor from
`0.00084D` to `0.00119D` by retaining total majority mass in the audited Mantel-shore
argument. The dominant atomic orbit has additional structure: it contains at most three
projective direction classes, and its physical crossing graph is triangle-free.
Applying the exact three-partite phase-star inequality before generic Mantel-shore
assignment gives a substantially stronger bound:

\[
\boxed{
\liminf_{D\to\infty}{\sigma_R\over D}\ge0.0018,
\qquad
\sigma_R:=2T_R-4\sqrt{R_4}.
}
\tag{PSG.1}
\]

Here `T_R=B_4/2` is the non-singleton strict-rhombus train count. The scalar root of
the displayed argument is about `0.00182215`; `0.0018` is used as a safe round value.
The outward certificate leaves a projected-diameter margin greater than `0.00149`.

This theorem supersedes the numerical conclusion of
`target_c_sharpened_rhombic_train_gap_2026-09-14.md`, while that note remains a valid
intermediate proof.

## 1. Small train slack controls the whole atomic orbit ledger

Put

\[
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
\qquad r:=\sqrt{\Delta_2},
\qquad s_0:=0.0018.
\]

Assume for contradiction that a full-core Type-II subsequence has

\[
{\sigma_R\over D}\to s<s_0.
\tag{PSG.2}
\]

Since `R_4/D^2 -> Delta_2`, the identity defining `sigma_R` gives

\[
{T_R\over D}\to2r+{s\over2}
< x_0:=2r+{s_0\over2}.
\tag{PSG.3}
\]

Pass to the rhombic-train direction-measure limit already justified in
`target_c_explicit_rhombic_train_gap_2026-09-14.md`. Let `A_R` be its absolute atomic
mass in `D` normalization. Group its atoms into the at-most-three-point projective
orbits generated by addition of `pi/3`. If `W_j` is orbit mass and `E_j` its crossing
mass, then

\[
E_j\le {W_j^2\over4},
\qquad
\sum_j E_j=\Delta_2,
\qquad
\sum_j W_j=A_R,
\qquad
2r\le A_R\le x_0.
\tag{PSG.4}
\]

Let `W` be the largest orbit mass. The standard Section-166 estimate gives

\[
W\ge {4\Delta_2\over A_R}
\ge {4\Delta_2\over x_0}
=:W_0.
\tag{PSG.5}
\]

Define the total atomic Mantel gap

\[
G:={A_R^2\over4}-\Delta_2.
\]

The exact orbit decomposition used in Sections 166/168 writes `G` as the sum of
nonnegative cross-orbit mass-spread and individual orbit Mantel deficits. Therefore,
for the dominant orbit,

\[
\delta:={W^2\over4}-E\le G
\le {x_0^2\over4}-\Delta_2
=:d_0.
\tag{PSG.6}
\]

As before,

\[
d_0={s_0r\over2}+{s_0^2\over16}.
\tag{PSG.7}
\]

## 2. Use the three phase classes directly

Freeze shrinking continuity windows around the at most three atoms of the dominant
orbit and pass far enough along the realizing finite source sequence. Up to `o(D)` and
`o(D^2)` errors, write the three window populations as

\[
C\ge U\ge V\ge0,
\qquad C+U+V=W,
\tag{PSG.8}
\]

and let their crossing graph have `E` edges in `D^2` normalization. Same-window trains
do not cross, and the physical rhombic train graph is triangle-free.

The elementary three-partite phase-star inequality, already proved in the repository,
is

\[
E\le C(U+V)=C(W-C).
\tag{PSG.9}
\]

Combining with `E=W^2/4-delta` gives

\[
\boxed{
\left(C-{W\over2}\right)^2\le\delta\le d_0.
}
\tag{PSG.10}
\]

Write

\[
z_0:=\sqrt{d_0}.
\]

Then

\[
C\ge {W\over2}-z_0,
\qquad
U\ge {W-C\over2}\ge {W\over4}-{z_0\over2}.
\tag{PSG.11}
\]

In particular the center class and the larger leaf class satisfy

\[
\boxed{
C+U\ge {3W\over4}-{z_0\over2}
\ge {3W_0\over4}-{z_0\over2}.
}
\tag{PSG.12}
\]

This avoids the `sqrt(6d)` minority-assignment loss entirely.

## 3. The center-to-large-leaf missing budget is also controlled

Let

\[
x:=CU-e(C,U),
\qquad y:=CV-e(C,V),
\qquad e_{UV}:=e(U,V)
\]

be the two center-leaf missing counts and the leaf-leaf crossing count, all normalized
by `D^2`. The proof of the tripartite phase-star inequality gives the stronger relation

\[
C e_{UV}\le Vx+Uy.
\tag{PSG.13}
\]

Put

\[
g:=C(U+V)-E=x+y-e_{UV}.
\]

Since

\[
g=\delta-\left(C-{W\over2}\right)^2,
\]

we have `0<=g<=d_0`. From (PSG.13),

\[
\begin{aligned}
g
&\ge x\left(1-{V\over C}\right)
  +y\left(1-{U\over C}\right)\\
&\ge x\left(1-{V\over C}\right).
\end{aligned}
\tag{PSG.14}
\]

Because `V<=(W-C)/2` and `C>=W/2-z_0`,

\[
1-{V\over C}
\ge
{W/2-3z_0\over W-2z_0}.
\tag{PSG.15}
\]

The last expression increases with `W` and decreases with `z_0`, so using `W>=W_0`
we may set

\[
q_0:={W_0/2-3z_0\over W_0-2z_0}>0
\tag{PSG.16}
\]

and conclude

\[
\boxed{x\le m_0:={d_0\over q_0}.}
\tag{PSG.17}
\]

Thus the selected center/large-leaf product has a quantitative missing-crossing budget
without passing through a generic Mantel partition.

## 4. Reuse the audited robust-grid pruning

Put

\[
p_0:=\sqrt{m_0}.
\tag{PSG.18}
\]

The two selected direction windows shrink to distinct atoms of the same orbit, so their
axes differ by projective angle exactly `pi/3`.

Run the tie-safe Section-163 phase-order argument with absolute threshold `p_0D`.
Every phase inversion is a missing center-leaf pair, and (PSG.17) shows there are at
most `(p_0^2+o(1))D^2` such pairs. Hence phase separation deletes at most
`p_0D+o(D)` trains from each family.

Then delete every train missing more than `p_0D+o(D)` opposite trains. The same budget
deletes at most another `p_0D+o(D)` trains per family. The retained family sizes obey

\[
{|C'|\over D}
\ge {W_0\over2}-z_0-2p_0-o(1),
\tag{PSG.19}
\]

\[
{|U'|\over D}
\ge {W_0\over4}-{z_0\over2}-2p_0-o(1).
\tag{PSG.20}
\]

The companion certificate proves

\[
{W_0\over4}-{z_0\over2}>5p_0,
\tag{PSG.21}
\]

so both retained shores are large enough that every pair and every triple of trains on
one shore has a common neighbor on the other. The audited actual-`K_{2,2}` consistency
and triple-neighbor transitivity from Section 163 therefore produce global crossing
orders.

## 5. Opposite-corner diameter contradiction

The Section-163 opposite-corner path loses at most one additional `p_0D+o(D)` degree
at each endpoint. Combining this with (PSG.12) and the four train-population pruning
losses gives a selected degree sum at least

\[
\boxed{
E_0D-o(D),
\qquad
E_0:={3W_0\over4}-{z_0\over2}-6p_0.
}
\tag{PSG.22}
\]

The two phase windows shrink to axes at angle `pi/3`, so the common projection of each
selected unit train vector tends uniformly to `sqrt(3)/2`. Hence the full source
diameter would satisfy

\[
1\ge {\sqrt3\over2}E_0-o(1).
\tag{PSG.23}
\]

At `s_0=0.0018`, the outward certificate proves

\[
E_0>{2\over\sqrt3}+0.0017
\tag{PSG.24}
\]

and

\[
{\sqrt3\over2}E_0>1.00149.
\tag{PSG.25}
\]

This contradiction proves (PSG.1).

## 6. Consequences and relation to earlier gaps

The exact rationalization

\[
M_R={\sigma_R\over8}
\left(4\sqrt{R_4}+{\sigma_R\over2}\right)
\]

now gives the safe quadratic deficit floor

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {M_R\over D^2}
\ge {0.0018\over8}
\left(4\sqrt{\Delta_2}+{0.0018\over2}\right)
>0.000857.
}
\tag{PSG.26}
\]

For comparison:

- the AG42-doubled floor was `sigma_R/D >= 0.00084`;
- the majority-mass refinement proved `sigma_R/D >= 0.00119`;
- (PSG.1) proves `sigma_R/D >= 0.0018`.

The new bound is more than twice the `0.00084` value and raises the unavoidable
quadratic missing-rhombus-crossing density from about `0.0004000` to more than
`0.000857`.

It can be substituted into any downstream argument that uses only a universal
rhombic train-slack or Mantel-deficit lower bound. It does not eliminate the large
rhombic-defect branch: `0.0018D` remains far below the `0.1679D` scale in the current
detour-free exceptional-train gate.

## 7. Scope audit

- The proof uses `T_R`, the non-singleton rhombic train count. Singleton complete
  stopped trains are never inserted into the crossing graph.
- The measure-limit relation support, atomic orbit decomposition, and finite-window
  extraction are exactly the rhombic-only transfer already justified in the explicit
  rhombic-train theorem.
- The three-partite inequality is applied only to the at-most-three atom windows of one
  projective `pi/3` orbit; same-window crossings are forbidden and the physical train
  graph is triangle-free.
- Equation (PSG.13) is the same neighborhood-disjointness inequality used in the
  repository's phase-star refinement; it is not a new graph theorem imported from
  outside the project.
- Phase separation, degree pruning, crossing-order globalization, and the source
  opposite-corner path are the audited Section-163 mechanisms.
- Atomic-mass semicontinuity is not used: continuity windows are frozen before finite
  sources are selected.
- Contact-isolated source points remain in the full source.
- No macroscopic inactive-container conclusion is claimed.
- No exact unit triangle or same-cardinality strict sub-`D` competitor is produced.

**Disposition:** new proof-only candidate; independent audit required. C-II and Level I
remain open.
